---
/* THE PRIVACY NOTICE. Articles 13 and 14 GDPR for the site, section 25 TDDDG
   for anything that would touch the reader's device, and one section for the
   application because the reader who came here came to ask about that.

   IT DESCRIBES THE REAL STACK AND NOTHING ELSE. The inventory it is written
   against is small and was read out of the repository rather than remembered:
   Cloudflare serves the site and resolves its name, the four typefaces are
   served from this origin, the four outbound links in the footer are links and
   not embeds, and `src/` contains no `localStorage`, no `sessionStorage`, no
   `document.cookie` and no `indexedDB` -- checked, not assumed. A service
   named here that does not run is the same defect as a service that runs and
   is not named.

   THE APPLICATION SECTION IS NOT DECORATION. A dictation product's privacy
   page that only discusses its marketing site answers the question nobody
   asked. What the app does with audio is the question, the FAQ answers it in
   one line, and this is where that line is allowed to be exact. Every claim in
   it is read off the runtime: `src-tauri/src` carries no telemetry client, and
   its only outbound hosts are the transcription providers and the GitHub
   releases endpoint that `core::updates` calls.

   THE THIRD COUNTRY PARAGRAPH IS DELIBERATELY DOUBLE. The EU-US Data Privacy
   Framework is in force and Cloudflare is certified under it, and an appeal
   against the adequacy decision is pending before the Court of Justice after
   the General Court dismissed the first challenge on 2025-09-03. Naming the
   standard contractual clauses alongside it means this page does not have to
   be rewritten in a hurry on the day that appeal is decided.

   ON SECTION 25 TDDDG: the statute is the TDDDG and has been since 2024. A
   page that still calls it the TTDSG is citing a name that was replaced, the
   same class of defect as an imprint that cites the TMG. */
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';
import { AUTHORITY, ENTITY, HOST } from '../lib/legal';
import { LINKS } from '../lib/linkMarks';
import { LICENCE_URL, SITE } from '../lib/site';
import { BEACON } from '../lib/analytics';

/* The outbound set, named from the module the footer draws it from, so a link
   added to the row cannot go unnamed here. */
const OUTBOUND = LINKS.map((l) => l.label).join(', ');
---
<Legal
  title="Privacy"
  lede="What this site processes, what the application does with what you say, and the rights you have over both."
>
  <h2>Controller</h2>
  <p>The controller for the processing described here is:</p>
  <address>
    {ENTITY.name}<br />
    {ENTITY.holder}<br />
    {ENTITY.street}<br />
    {ENTITY.postcode} {ENTITY.city}<br />
    {ENTITY.country}<br />
    Email: <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>
  </address>

  <h2>What this notice covers</h2>
  <p>
    Two different things, kept apart because they behave differently. The first
    part is this website. The second part is the {SITE.name} application, which
    runs on your own machine and which we never see.
  </p>

  {/* THE SUMMARY MOVES WITH THE MEASUREMENT. Both halves of this paragraph
      read the same boolean the beacon does, because a summary that promises a
      section which is not there is the same defect as the section itself. */}
  <h2>This website in one paragraph</h2>
  <p>
    It sets no cookies, stores nothing on your device, has no accounts, no
    forms and no embedded third-party content. Nothing on it profiles you and
    nothing follows you to another site. What remains is the connection data
    that reaching any web server produces{BEACON
      ? ', plus a reach measurement that does not touch your device. Both are described below.'
      : ', and it is described below.'}
  </p>

  <h2>Hosting, content delivery and DNS</h2>
  <p>
    This site is delivered by {HOST.name}, {HOST.address}, which also resolves
    its domain name. All traffic between your browser and this site therefore
    passes over Cloudflare's network. Cloudflare processes technical connection
    data, including your IP address, in order to deliver the site, to keep it
    available and to defend it against attacks.
  </p>
  <p>
    The legal basis is our legitimate interest in providing this site securely
    and efficiently, Article 6 (1) (f) GDPR. A data processing agreement under
    Article 28 GDPR is in place with the provider. Transfers to the United
    States are covered by Cloudflare's certification under the EU-US Data
    Privacy Framework and, in addition, by standard contractual clauses. Their
    own notice is at <a href={HOST.privacy}>{HOST.privacy}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Server log files</h2>
  <p>
    We operate no web server of our own for this site and we neither receive
    nor keep access logs. The connection data described in the previous section
    arises at Cloudflare and is retained by Cloudflare under its own policy. We
    have no access to it in a form that identifies you.
  </p>

  {/* THIS SECTION IS CONDITIONAL, AND THE REASON IS A DEFECT THAT WAS LIVE.
      It stood here in full -- what is processed, how it is hashed, six months
      of availability -- while nothing was being measured, because the edge
      injection it was written for does not reach a Workers static-assets
      response. It now renders off the same value that renders the beacon, so
      the two cannot drift apart again. src/lib/analytics.ts, ADR 0261. */}
  {BEACON && (
  <Fragment>
  <h2>Reach measurement</h2>
  <p>
    We use Cloudflare Web Analytics to see how this site is used and how
    quickly it loads. The service sets no cookies and stores nothing on your
    device. It does not build profiles across devices and does not recognize
    you on other sites.
  </p>
  <p>
    Processed are: the pages requested, the referring page, country, browser,
    operating system and load-speed measurements. IP address, user agent and
    further request headers are used to tell one visit from another, are hashed
    immediately in the process and are not stored. Query strings are not
    logged. The data is available to us for six months.
  </p>
  <p>
    The legal basis is our legitimate interest in a site that is shaped by how
    it is actually used, Article 6 (1) (f) GDPR. Because nothing is stored on
    or read from your device, no consent under section 25 TDDDG is required and
    this site therefore has no cookie banner.
  </p>
  <p>
    The script that performs it is served by Cloudflare from
    <code>static.cloudflareinsights.com</code>, which is the same provider that
    already delivers this site and is named as such above. No further party is
    involved.
  </p>
  </Fragment>
  )}

  <h2>Typefaces</h2>
  <p>
    Every typeface this site uses is served from this domain. No request goes
    to a font provider and no connection to a third party is opened in order to
    render text.
  </p>

  <h2>Links to other sites</h2>
  <p>
    We link to {OUTBOUND}. These are ordinary links, not embedded content:
    nothing is transferred to those providers before you click one, and no
    request to them exists while you are only reading this site. Once you
    follow a link you have left this site, and the provider's own terms and
    privacy notice apply from that point.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact by email</h2>
  <p>
    If you write to us, your message and the details in it are processed in
    order to answer it and to deal with follow-up questions. The legal basis is
    Article 6 (1) (b) GDPR where your enquiry relates to a contract or its
    initiation, and otherwise our legitimate interest in answering enquiries
    addressed to us, Article 6 (1) (f) GDPR. We keep correspondence for as long
    as the matter it concerns is open, and delete it afterwards unless a
    statutory retention period applies to it.
  </p>

  <h2>The {SITE.name} application</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} is a desktop application. It runs on your machine, it has no
    account and no sign-up, and there is no server of ours behind it. It
    contains no telemetry, no usage reporting and no crash reporting: it sends
    us nothing, and we hold no data about how you use it.
  </p>
  <p>
    What you dictate is written to a directory you chose, on your own disk. The
    application also keeps a runtime log and a record of its own operations
    there. None of it leaves the machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    Audio leaves your machine only if you point a profile at a cloud model.
    Then that profile's audio and the text made from it go to the vendor you
    selected, over your own account and your own key, and that vendor's terms
    and privacy notice govern what happens to it. We are not a party to that
    relationship and we receive nothing from it. The choice is made per
    profile, not once for the whole application, and a profile on the local
    lane reaches no network at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your provider key is held in your operating system's own secret store and
    is stripped from the application's configuration file whenever that file is
    written.
  </p>
  <p>
    The application makes one request of its own accord: when you first open
    its About section, and again whenever you ask it to check, it queries the
    GitHub releases endpoint for this project to find out whether a newer
    version exists. That request reaches GitHub and carries your IP address and
    the version you are running. It does not run at startup and it runs for no
    other section.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>You have the right at any time to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>access the data held about you, Article 15 GDPR</li>
    <li>have inaccurate data corrected, Article 16 GDPR</li>
    <li>have data erased, Article 17 GDPR</li>
    <li>have processing restricted, Article 18 GDPR</li>
    <li>receive your data in a portable form, Article 20 GDPR</li>
    <li>withdraw a consent you gave, with effect for the future, Article 7 (3) GDPR</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a> to exercise any
    of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Right to object</h2>
  <p>
    Where we process data on the basis of legitimate interests under Article 6
    (1) (f) GDPR, you may object at any time on grounds arising from your
    particular situation, Article 21 GDPR. We will then stop processing the
    data unless we can demonstrate compelling legitimate grounds that override
    your interests, rights and freedoms.
  </p>

  <h2>Right to complain</h2>
  <p>
    You may complain to a supervisory authority, Article 77 GDPR. The authority
    responsible for the private sector at our seat is the{' '}
    <a href={AUTHORITY.url}>{AUTHORITY.name}</a>, {AUTHORITY.street},{' '}
    {AUTHORITY.postcode} {AUTHORITY.city}, {AUTHORITY.country}. You may also
    approach the authority in your own country of residence or place of work.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this notice</h2>
  <p>
    We update this notice when what it describes changes: a service added or
    removed, a change in the entity behind the site, or a change in the law it
    rests on. The date at the foot of this page is when the whole of it was
    last checked against the site and the application, not when a word in it
    last moved. The source of both is public, and the licence is{' '}
    <a href={LICENCE_URL}>AGPL-3.0</a>: what this page describes can be
    verified rather than taken on trust.
  </p>
</Legal>
